# Turtle is compared byte-for-byte by the semantic baseline, so its line
# endings are part of the artifact. LF everywhere, on every platform.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf decided the bytes: a Windows checkout produced
# CRLF, a Linux one LF, and the two disagreed about the contents of every
# multi-line literal, because a CRLF inside one is part of that literal's
# value. Measured over the whole corpus, that is 77 literal-object triples
# in 8 files (config/eol-transition-matrix.json, line_ending_transition).
#
# 11 of those 77 are sh:select -- SPARQL text a validator executes, not
# prose somebody reads. The rest are bibRef, rdfs:comment, skos notes and
# owl:versionInfo. So this is not only a cosmetic difference in annotations.
#
# The byte digests recorded before this rule describe the machine that
# measured them.
*.ttl text eol=lf
